4.  mutation controls
====================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation: shuffling g_obs across points destroys the relation for both kernels, so S1/S2 are structure in the data and not in the binning   (shuffled residual power far exceeds the real one)
  [PASS] M2 limiting check: the kernels agree asymptotically (deep MOND: g = sqrt(g_bar a_0); Newtonian: g = g_bar), so S2 probes their transition as well as fixed-input normalization   (both limits agree to <0.005 dex)

====================================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================================
  The repository has been running two kernels and calling them one.  The empirical nu_RAR = 1/(1-e^{-sqrt y}) and
  the field-theory 'exact exponential' mu_exp = 1-e^{-x} share both limits and differ by up to ~0.08 dex in the
  transition. At fixed inputs, the diagonal-bin statistic differs by 163 on 18 bins, with
  a largest standardized transition residual of 7.5 for mu_exp. These are conditional diagnostics,
  not a calibrated rejection: cross-bin covariance, a0 fitting and galaxy nuisance parameters are absent.
  The two_kernel_orbit_shape_2026 audit profiles a0 and resamples complete galaxies in paired draws.
  A kernel substitution is a new action target; neither relativistic closure nor a PPN coefficient transfers automatically.
  For nu_RAR's algebraic curve, the theoretical maximum g_ph is 0.648 a0 at g_bar=2.54 a0.
  The selected observed peak-window median is 8.83 +/- 3.79 x 1e-11 m/s^2.
  This median does not certify a bound on individual catalog estimates. The a0-Lambda relation remains an input.

